the conflict in ukraine has shone a  light on the uk's pre-existing energy  issues uk consumers are facing pretty  massive increases to their bills we have  obviously seen a big push into solar and  wind energy  but what a lot of people now say is  that's not going to be enough to meet  the uk's ambitious decarbonization  targets and as a result nuclear has come  back into favor bringing nuclear home  with one nuclear plant and one nuclear  reactor every year for eight years  rather than one a decade so i went to  anglesey which is an island off the  north west coast of wales they have a  nuclear reactor which is in the process  of decommissioning  and that site is one of the top  contenders for one of the first modular  reactors they're now talking about 25  of the uk's energy  coming from nuclear by 2050 anglesey has  been known as one of the least  productive places in the uk that is  partly because there's quite an aging  population there a lot of retirees and a  lot of young people leave to find work  elsewhere  small modular reactors are basically as  it sounds smaller versions of  traditional reactors importantly they're  modular which means they can be built  away from site in factories and then  transported to the site and what that  does is it brings down the costs so in  the uk the company that's  looking to make these reactors is rolls  royce they do have experience in nuclear  through their work  on the uk's nuclear submarines what the  supporters say  is that you know these are smaller than  the previous technology they're also  much newer technology and therefore they  argue that there is less risk a  government official said that a  breakdown in an atomic power plant in  pennsylvania today is probably the worst  nuclear reactor accident to date nuclear  has had safety concerns  stretching back decades and those aren't  going away and i mean personally i  didn't feel any risk from that but when  we spoke to some of the experts there  obviously  that safety risk is a big concern for  locals small modular reactors are  effectively an untested technology and  quite a lot of people in the industry  who are maybe a bit more skeptical refer  to these as powerpoint reactors because  none of them are are up and running yet  but i think broadly there is quite a lot  of support the local mp  ran on a platform to get the nuclear  site reopened the majority of my  constituents support wilvernowth it's  recognized as the best site in the uk  possibly the world to host a nuclear  power plant the most powerful argument i  heard for smrs was to do with the jobs  benefit i heard from one of the sources  that there are taxi drivers for example  on anglesey who were actually qualified  nuclear engineers but when the plants  decommissioned they didn't want to leave  the local area and so they've had to  move into into something else a lot of  local people have worked in nuclear they  have experience with it they've lived  near the plan and what was really  interesting is that you can see the  welsh government has already made some  efforts to  support the local economy around that  plant even while it's decommissioning so  there's there's quite a nice cafe  there's a coastal path so it's good to  kind of see a different image of nuclear  than what we might think of the most  powerful argument against i think is a  combination of the safety and the  environmental concerns and one thing i  heard from a couple of people i spoke to  was a sense that the westminster  government is quite happy to  encourage nuclear in places that are  quite far away from london  um you know a sense that they'll they'll  put it in wales but they won't put it on  their own back door  [Music]  you
 

